1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,864
Meet Gummy Books, a back office for a book shop.

2
00:00:04,814 --> 00:00:11,209
It has a dashboard, server-paged tables with filters, detail pages, forms and sign-in.

3
00:00:15,371 --> 00:00:18,132
And not one of these screens was written by hand.

4
00:00:18,640 --> 00:00:21,274
Every page was designed in Gummy Studio.

5
00:00:24,006 --> 00:00:31,454
Drop a component on the canvas, point it at an API endpoint, and set paging, sorting and filters in its properties.

6
00:00:33,869 --> 00:00:37,201
Then open the live preview, and try it with real data.

7
00:00:37,902 --> 00:00:41,006
Sign-in is a shared entry that each project picks.

8
00:00:42,767 --> 00:00:48,163
Roles are plain conditions, like: show this button only when the signed-in role is manager.

9
00:00:49,026 --> 00:00:52,654
Menus, colours and page transitions are settings too.

10
00:00:53,202 --> 00:00:58,007
A clerk opens an order with a pending refund, and sees it's waiting for a manager.

11
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:02,001
The manager opens the same order, and can approve it.

12
00:01:06,335 --> 00:01:08,951
One page, one condition apart.

13
00:01:09,654 --> 00:01:12,849
The studio saves plain JSON next to your code.

14
00:01:12,749 --> 00:01:16,022
Change the app bar title, and the running app follows.

15
00:01:17,501 --> 00:01:22,069
Gummy Studio. Design the screens, and keep your code for what matters.
